  "executive_briefing": "The corporate and international tax policy landscape in mid-2026 is marked by structural adjustments and rising geopolitical trade friction. While over 140 countries actively advance the OECD's Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax, domestic policy changes are introducing localized shifts, such as the US 'One Big Beautiful Bill Act' extending key tax provisions and regional corporate rate changes in Portugal and Slovenia. Simultaneously, international trade is hit by a wave of fresh tariff proposals, including proposed US import curbs targeting forced labor across dozens of countries, creating high-risk planning environments for global supply chains. In compliance, real-time transaction validations have matured into boardroom-level priorities, exemplified by Singapore's InvoiceNow Peppol BIS 3.0 mandate and India's expanding e-invoicing portal requirements.",
